requirements-lock.txt
# Generated from requirements-lock.txt by scripts/generate_runtime_lock.py; same
# content and the same reason as the entry above. py.numeric.float-equality fires on
# pins like `alembic==1.20.0`, reading the dotted version as float comparison. A
# version pin is string equality, never a numeric one, and this file has no arithmetic.
requirements-runtime-lock.txt
tests/
# The BEAM differential harness, for the same reason as `tests/` above: it is test
# scaffolding, not shipped code, and it exists to RUN the Python reference.
#
# Its findings are true and unavoidable rather than false positives, which is the
# stronger reason to scope it out. `parity_oracle.py` AST-lifts functions out of
# `app/` and executes them, so `py.security.eval-exec-usage` (3), `py.eval-exec` (3)
# and `python.taint.eval` (1) fire on `exec(compile(...))` — there is no other way to
# execute a lifted reference. The three `py.parsing.json-loads-no-try` warnings fire
# on `json.loads` of the oracle's own output, where a decode failure must raise
# rather than be swallowed.
#
# The `tests/` glob missed this path because it lives under `beam/apps/*/test/`, so
# without this line the gate's critical floor rises every time the harness grows —
# which is how a scaffolding file becomes a permanent tax on real findings. Scoped
# BY PATH, never by rule: a rule-level suppression would also silence the same rule
# in shipped code.
beam/apps/*/test/
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/
migrations/versions/
